thread:
jia: quick QA — seeing intermittent malformed JSON from streaming endpoints under high client-side backpressure. Repro locally by throttling the consumer read (sleep) after the socket is open; parser sometimes sees a fragment like '{\"id\":123, \"text\": \"partial' and throws. anyone hit a netty read-loop stall + partial-json concat issue?

samir: yup saw this pattern before. usually it's a combination of disabling AUTO_READ and doing a brittle line-based parse. netty can pause reads and you'll end up with fragmented events. are you using a non-blocking/incremental parser or just readLine() + JsonNode?

jia: currently it's BufferedReader.readLine() per SSE event and then ObjectMapper.readTree(line). server uses chunked transfer and sometimes doesn't flush the event delimiter, so readLine returns partial.

amanda: quick checklist: treat SSE event boundaries explicitly (\n\n delimiter), or switch to Jackson non-blocking parser. also ensure you don't hold onto ByteBuf without retaining/releasing. pseudo: ```
ByteBuf buf = Unpooled.buffer();
while (inbound.isReadable()) {
  buf.writeBytes(inbound.readBytes(inbound.readableBytes()));
  int idx = indexOfDoubleNewline(buf);
  if (idx >= 0) {
    ByteBuf ev = buf.readBytes(idx);
    handleEvent(ev.toString(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    buf.skipBytes(2); // consume delimiter
  } else {
    // not enough data yet -> apply backpressure policy if buf grows
    if (buf.readableBytes() > MAX_BUF) { /* drop/close/retry */ }
  }
}
```

samir: +1. Also important: when you manually manage auto-read, call ctx.channel().read() after you drain or free buffers. Example: if you set channel.config().setAutoRead(false) to apply backpressure, you must explicitly re-enable or call read() to resume. otherwise netty won't push more bytes and clients appear stuck.

ryan: on reconnect/backoff policy — how aggressive should client retries be? right now we do immediate reconnects which cause thundering herd and worsens SLOs.

samir: recommend jittered exponential backoff with a short immediate window for transient hiccups. e.g. try immediate reconnects for up to 3s total, then exponential backoff starting at 250ms with jitter, cap at ~30s. on reconnect, try to resume from last cursor if the server supports it, otherwise fetch a small warmup range to prime KV cache.

dev-bot: deploy: platform/streaming-parser-fix -> canary started :rocket: (build 2026-03-17T15:01Z)

jia: one more strange thing — when I pause the consumer thread (Thread.sleep) I sometimes get RC leak warnings (io.netty.util.IllegalReferenceCountException). stack snippet shows ByteBuf touch traces. any quick mitigation?

amanda: use SimpleChannelInboundHandler<T> if you want netty to auto-release messages for you, or ensure ReferenceCountUtil.release(msg) after you copy needed bytes. also beware of retaining buffers into async queues: if you keep a ByteBuf across futures, call retain() and later release. switching to copyToString for the event (i.e., new String(bytes, UTF_8)) avoids retaining pooled buffers.

jia: ok switching to SimpleChannelInboundHandler + copying event bytes into a byte[] before handing to Jackson non-blocking parser. will also add a bounded queue + drop policy and the jittered backoff.

